<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tajiks</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tajiks are a Persian-speaking people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, living mainly in Tajikistan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. They descend largely from the Iranian population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and Sogdiana, and cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> were centres of their culture. The Soviet borders drawn in the 1920s left many Tajiks outside the Tajik republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tajiks.jpg" alt="Tajiks" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ilhoms · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Happy_Tajik_children.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Iron Age people of Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan drew about 57% of their ancestry from Andronovo-related steppe herders and about 43% from the BMAC population, and modern <b>Tajiks</b> and Yaghnobis are found to be direct descendants of these Bronze and Iron Age Central Asians.</blockquote><a href="/culture/oxus-civilisation">Oxus civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 19th century their leader Murad Beg built a powerful state from Kunduz, taking Balkh, Badakhshan and neighbouring <b>Tajik</b> districts around 1815 to 1820.</blockquote><a href="/state/kataghan-khanate">Kataghan Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Its borders, drawn in 1924, left large Uzbek and <b>Tajik</b> communities inside the republic and Kyrgyz communities outside it.</blockquote><a href="/state/kirghiz-assr">Kirghiz ASSR</a></li><li><blockquote>The mirs ruled a mountainous land of <b>Tajik</b> farmers and herders, including Ismaili communities in the Pamirs and Sunni Muslims in the lowlands.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-badakhshan">Principality of Badakhshan</a></li><li><blockquote>The Samanids are remembered in Tajikistan as founders of <b>Tajik</b> statehood.</blockquote><a href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Habibullah Kalakani, a <b>Tajik</b> bandit leader from the Shomali plain north of Kabul, known as Bacha-i Saqao, 'son of the water carrier', captured Kabul in January 1929, and Amanullah abdicated and fled.</blockquote><a href="/state/saqqawist-emirate">Saqqawist Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
